{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"Multiplying the conditional density by f_X(x) cancels 3x²+1, giving f(x,y)=3(x²+y²)/2 on the unit square and zero elsewhere. This integrates to 1 and agrees with MA's worked solution. No learner grade is supplied.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"answer","correct_value":"\\frac{3(x^{2}+y^{2})}{2}","correct_option":null,"value_type":"math","wrong_value":"\\frac{x^{2}+y^{2}}{2}","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"\\frac{3(x^{2}+y^{2})}{2}","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{2}.\\end{aligned}","disagrees":false}]}